# Codex Adversarial Review

Target: branch diff against origin/main
Verdict: needs-attention

Do not ship yet. Static review of clean head bbded20 against origin/main 40b484b: R-006 and R-009 remain incomplete—plain newline continuation, repeated blank lines, single-line pipelines, and sequential cd tracking hold, but pipeline comments bypass tracking. R-008 remains incomplete because sed write/read classification still fails. R-011 fixes the recorded 1w, unaddressed gw, and long-option examples; sed_arg_targets extracts --expression=/--file= values, but addressed substitutions still bypass detection. R-012's attached-script false block is fixed, and bare direct-path sed -i still blocks through Python alone; removing the pre-filter nevertheless introduces the coverage regression below. R-013 fixes warning/flow/raw examples but still blocks ordinary regex text. Additional separated/clustered cd options and short sed value options e/f/l trace correctly. New findings: one pre-filter-removal regression; three remaining finding variants detailed below. Read-only Git/file inspection commands exited 0; no files edited or builds/tests run. Behavioral conclusions are static traces, not runtime reproductions.

Findings:
- [high] New: removing the sed pre-filter permits prefixed in-place writes (.claude/hooks/guard-oracle.sh:64-65)
  From the repository root, `env sed -i 's/a/b/' tests/golden/nesc/case1_dragless_sphere.hpp` previously matched the coarse sed alternative. It now reaches command_arg_targets, whose verb is `env`, so it returns no targets and the hook allows the oracle edit. `/usr/bin/sed -i ...` and `(sed -i ... tests/golden/nesc/case1_dragless_sphere.hpp)` similarly miss the exact `verb == "sed"` check without causing shlex to fail. Thus the removed filter was not redundant. Bare `sed -i ...` does still block: Python recognizes -i and resolves the literal file into tests/golden.
  Recommendation: Preserve detection for executable paths, ordinary command prefixes, and subshell-wrapped sed before removing fallback coverage; retain the attached -e read-only cases.
- [high] R-006/R-009: a pipeline comment still resets the tracked parent directory (.claude/hooks/guard-oracle.sh:121-129)
  For `cd tests/golden; printf x | # continuation\ncd ../..; printf x > probe.hpp` (with an actual newline), Bash keeps cd in the pipeline, leaving the parent inside tests/golden. The splitter puts the comment into buf, so the whitespace-only continuation condition fails and flushes a semicolon boundary. The following cd is then treated as sequential, moving cur to the repository root. The final protected write is allowed. Multiple blank lines without comments work, but ordinary pipeline comments reopen the same bypass.
  Recommendation: Recognize shell comments outside quotes and preserve pending pipeline membership across comment-only continuation lines.
- [high] R-008/R-011: digit-addressed substitutions evade both write regexes (.claude/hooks/guard-oracle.sh:228-239)
  `cd tests/golden && sed -n '1s/x/y/gw probe.hpp' /dev/null` is classified as read-only. The bare-command regex rejects w because g precedes it; the substitution regex requires a word boundary before s, which does not exist between 1 and s. Sed's valid addressed substitution opens the output file, potentially truncating an existing protected file even with empty input. Attached expressions such as `-es/x/y/gw probe.hpp` also lack the required boundary before s.
  Recommendation: Extract expression values before classification and recognize substitution commands after valid sed addresses rather than using a word boundary as the command boundary.
- [medium] R-008/R-013: regex text containing a separated w remains falsely blocked (.claude/hooks/guard-oracle.sh:228-239)
  `sed -n '/w probe/p' tests/golden/nesc/case1_dragless_sphere.hpp` only reads and prints, but SED_BARE_W_CMD_RE matches `w probe` inside the address regex. That classifies the invocation as write-capable, after which its protected input filename causes a block. Requiring whitespace fixes the recorded words but does not satisfy the requested distinction between regex/literal regions and actual write commands.
  Recommendation: Exclude address regexes and literal-text regions when recognizing bare w/W commands; retain read-only cases containing whitespace after w.

